<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abaqa Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1234">1234</a>–<a href="/years/1282">1282</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Abaqa, Abagha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abaqa Khan was the second ruler of the Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, reigning from 1265 to 1282 as successor of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and sought alliances with Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the Mamluk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> invaded Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 1277, he executed the minister known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane">Pervane</a> and imposed direct Mongol control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan.jpg" alt="Abaqa Khan" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid-al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abaqa_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
